.he "awk (1)                     8/19/26                      awk (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
awk
- pattern scanning and processing language
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
awk [ -Fc ] [ -f progfile | 'program' ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Awk scans each input file for lines that match any of a set of patterns
specified in the program, which appears literally on the command line
or in a file named by -f progfile.  With each pattern there can be an
associated action, performed when a line matches.  Files are read in
order; with no files, the standard input is read.  A file name of -
means the standard input, and an argument of the form var=value is
treated as an assignment rather than a file name.
.sp
An input line is made up of fields separated by white space (changeable
with FS or -Fc).  The fields are $1, $2, ...; $0 is the whole line, and
$expr refers to a field indirectly.  NF, NR and FILENAME are the number
of fields in the current record, the number of records read so far, and
the name of the current input file.
.sp
A pattern-action statement has the form:
.nf
	pattern { action }
.fi
.sp
A missing { action } prints the line; a missing pattern always matches.
An action is a sequence of statements - if, while, for, break, continue,
next, exit, assignment, print, printf and getline - terminated by
semicolons, newlines or right braces.  Variables are created on first
use and take string or numeric values as appropriate; array subscripts
are strings, so arrays give associative memory.
.sp
Patterns are boolean combinations of regular expressions (as in egrep,
surrounded by slashes) and relational expressions (the six C relations,
plus ~ and !~ for match).  A pattern of two expressions separated by a
comma selects the range of lines between them.  BEGIN and END match
before the first input line and after the last.
.sp
The builtin functions are length, substr, index, split, sprintf, int,
log, exp and sqrt.  Numbers in this awk are 32-bit signed integers:
division truncates toward zero, and log, exp and sqrt return integer
approximations.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
Print lines longer than 72 characters:
.nf
	length > 72
.fi
.sp
Print the first two fields in opposite order:
.nf
	{ print $2, $1 }
.fi
.sp
Sum the first column, print sum and average:
.nf
	{ s += $1 }
	END { print "sum is", s, " average is", s/NR }
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sed (1), grep (1), lex (1)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
There is no floating point; numbers are 32-bit signed integers.
.sp
getline has no var or < file forms, and a relational expression is not
a general expression, so it can appear only in an if(...) test or a
pattern.  NF is 0 in an END block (the empty record read at end of
file is re-split).
